  },
  "generalizedRange": "Chukotka, Russia to the coasts of Southeast Asia",
  "hook": "A sparrow-sized bird with a spoon for a bill — and 8,000 km to fly.",
  "story": [
    {
      "id": "spoonie-unique",
      "text": "No shorebird on Earth looks like it: a bird the size of a sparrow with a black spoon at the end of its face.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "birdlife-saving-spoonie-2022",
        "eaafp-sbs-species-profile"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "spoonie-shovel",
      "text": "It uses the spoon as a shovel, lifting wet sand and mud in search of invertebrates.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "birdlife-saving-spoonie-2022"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "spoonie-plumage",
      "text": "In breeding plumage its head and breast flame reddish-brown above a white, black-spotted belly.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "eaafp-sbs-species-profile",
        "birdlife-saving-spoonie-2022"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "spoonie-flyway",
      "text": "Twice a year it flies 8,000 kilometers along the East Asian–Australasian Flyway, a superhighway shared with fifty million waterbirds.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "birdlife-saving-spoonie-2022"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "spoonie-reclamation",
      "text": "The flyway is losing its rest stops: when four hundred square kilometers of mudflat at Saemangeum were walled off, the hundreds of spoonies that stopped there fell to one.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "birdlife-saving-spoonie-2022"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "spoonie-nets",
      "text": "On the wintering grounds, young birds are caught in mist nets set for larger waders.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "birdlife-datazone-sbs-factsheet"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "spoonie-headstarting",
      "text": "So conservation follows the whole route. In Chukotka, eggs go into incubators and chicks into aviaries — headstarting that lifts their survival five-fold.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "birdlife-saving-spoonie-2022",
        "eaafp-sbs-task-force"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "spoonie-hunters",
      "text": "In Myanmar's Gulf of Mottama, more than 80 percent of hunters have agreed to stop, and report better livelihoods.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "birdlife-saving-spoonie-2022"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "spoonie-slowing",
      "text": "The decline has slowed from 26 percent a year to under ten — not saved, but no longer in freefall.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "birdlife-saving-spoonie-2022"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "spoonie-witness",
      "text": "To witness the spoon-billed sandpiper is to hold the whole flyway in mind — every mudflat a link in an 8,000-kilometer chain.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "birdlife-saving-spoonie-2022",
        "eaafp-sbs-task-force"
      ]
    }
  ],
